1. Frontend, Backend, and Full-Stack Development
The differences lie in the area of a website or application they address:
•	Frontend Development (Client-Side): Deals with the user interface (UI) and user experience (UX)—everything the user sees and interacts with directly in their web browser.
o	Focus: Layout, design, interactivity, and performance on the client's machine.
o	Languages/Technologies: HTML (structure), CSS (style), and JavaScript (interactivity).
o	Real-world Example: The visual layout of an Amazon product page, including the images, buttons (like "Add to Cart"), and the way a drop-down menu works.
•	Backend Development (Server-Side): Deals with the server, application logic, and database. It's the "behind-the-scenes" technology that handles data storage, security, and processing requests from the frontend.
o	Focus: Business logic, data storage, security, and communication with the frontend.
o	Languages/Technologies: Python (Django, Flask), Node.js (Express), PHP (Laravel), Java (Spring), etc., and databases like MySQL, PostgreSQL, MongoDB.
o	Real-world Example: When you click "Place Order" on Amazon, the backend processes the payment, checks inventory, updates the database, and sends an order confirmation to your email.
•	Full-Stack Development: Involves working with both the frontend and the backend. A full-stack developer is proficient in all layers of the application, from the user interface to the database and server logic.
o	Focus: Building and maintaining a complete, functional web application end-to-end.
o	Real-world Example: A developer who can build the interactive user profile page (frontend), write the server code to handle profile updates (backend), and design the database table to store the user's information (database).
