3. How a Browser Requests and Displays a Web Page
The process is a sequence of steps involving the browser, DNS, and the web server:
1.	User Input: The user types a URL (e.g., https://www.google.com) into the browser's address bar and presses Enter.
2.	DNS Lookup: The browser checks its cache to find the IP address corresponding to the domain name. If not found, it queries a DNS (Domain Name System) server (the "phonebook of the internet") to get the server's IP address.
3.	Establish Connection (TCP/IP/HTTP): The browser uses the IP address to initiate a connection (usually TCP/IP) with the web server. It then sends an HTTP/HTTPS request (e.g., a GET request for the main page) to the server.
4.	Server Processing: The web server receives the request, processes it (it might fetch data from a database if it's a dynamic site), and generates an appropriate HTTP response.
5.	Response Sent: The server sends the response back to the browser. This response includes the HTTP status code (e.g., 200 OK) and the requested data (HTML, CSS, JavaScript files, images, etc.).
6.	Rendering: The browser begins to render the page:
o	It first processes the HTML to build the DOM (Document Object Model), which defines the page structure.
o	It fetches and processes the CSS to build the CSSOM (CSS Object Model), which defines the style.
o	It combines the DOM and CSSOM to create the render tree and calculates the layout.
o	The page is painted onto the screen, and JavaScript is executed to add interactivity and final content.
