4. Tools for a Web Development Environment
A standard web development environment requires the following essential tools:
Tool	Purpose
Code Editor (e.g., VS Code)	A text editor designed specifically for writing and editing code. It provides features like syntax highlighting, code completion (IntelliSense), debugging, and integrated terminal, making coding faster and easier.
Web Browser (e.g., Chrome, Firefox)	Used to test and display the application. Includes essential Developer Tools (DevTools) for debugging code, inspecting elements (HTML/CSS), monitoring network activity, and profiling performance.
Command Line Interface (CLI)	A text-based interface used to interact with the operating system. It's necessary for running build scripts, installing packages (e.g., using npm or yarn), running local development servers, and managing Git.
Version Control System (Git)	A system to track changes to source code over time. It allows developers to collaborate, revert to previous versions, and manage different feature branches safely. GitHub or GitLab are popular hosting services for Git repositories.
Local Web Server (e.g., Live Server extension, Node.js)	A small server run locally on the developer's machine. This is crucial for testing dynamic functionality, making AJAX requests, and ensuring the application runs as it would on a production server.
Export to Sheets
