8. Static vs. Dynamic Websites
The difference lies in how and when the content is generated and delivered to the user.
Feature	Static Website	Dynamic Website
Content Generation	Content is pre-built and stored as fixed HTML files.	Content is generated on the fly when a user requests the page.
User Interaction	Limited interactivity; pages look the same for all users.	Highly interactive; content changes based on user, time, or database.
Technologies	Primarily HTML, CSS, and client-side JavaScript.	Backend languages (Python, PHP, Node.js, etc.), Databases, and HTML/CSS/JS.
Database Usage	None or minimal.	Essential for storing and retrieving personalized/changing data.
Export to Sheets
Examples:
•	Static Website Example: A simple personal portfolio or a corporate brochure site where the information rarely changes and is the same for every visitor.
•	Dynamic Website Example: Facebook (content changes with every user login), e-commerce sites (product availability, prices, user carts), or news websites (constantly updated content).
________________________________________
9. Five Web Browsers and Rendering Engines
Web browsers use rendering engines to interpret and display the HTML, CSS, and JavaScript code into the visual web page you see. Different browsers often use different engines.
Web Browser	Rendering Engine(s)	How Rendering Engines Differ
Google Chrome	Blink (A fork of WebKit)	Developed to optimize performance, memory management, and modern web standards compliance.
Mozilla Firefox	Gecko / Quantum	Known for its strong commitment to open standards and often pioneers new CSS features. Quantum is the modern, highly optimized version of Gecko.
Apple Safari	WebKit	Originally developed by Apple, it is lightweight, fast, and optimized for Apple's ecosystem (iOS/macOS).
Microsoft Edge	Blink (Since 2020; previously EdgeHTML)	Switched to Blink to improve compatibility with the majority of the web and leverage Google's development efforts.
Opera	Blink	Adopted the Blink engine (like Chrome) for better performance and compatibility after previously using Presto.
Export to Sheets
How Rendering Engines Differ:
The difference lies primarily in their implementation of web standards (HTML, CSS, JavaScript), their performance characteristics (speed, memory usage), and their compliance with specific specifications. These differences historically led to "browser wars" where a site looked different on one browser than another. Today, the dominance of a few engines (Blink, WebKit, Gecko) has led to much better cross-browser consistency, but minor variations in speed, security patches, and support for experimental features still exist.
